title: Recording a path
tags: [[File tracking]]
kind: reference
summary: A path's metarecord is created on request — refused when it exists or when a parent directory has none, unless the parents are asked for — with mf_watch = false, so the path is recorded but not followed.

`POST /repos/:repo/metarecords` with a `path` creates the metarecord of that file or
directory without the watcher or a reconcile: its `mfr_path` and stat fields are read
from disk, in one revision, beside whatever `fields` the request carries
([[Metarecord endpoints]]).

!! Recorded, not followed

The new metarecord gets `mf_watch = false` unless `fields` sets `mf_watch` itself. That
is what makes it worth having before anything is watched: a directory's `mf_ignore`
can be set on it first, and only then `mf_watch = true` and a reconcile
([[Tracking a folder]]). Inside a watched area the `false` is an explicit exclusion —
it holds the watcher off until the scope is decided. No eligibility rule is consulted:
deciding the watch scope is the caller's.

!! What is refused

* ''A path that already has its metarecord'': `409`, the existing uuid in the body's `uuid`. Creating is not finding — a client looking for the metarecord at a path asks a query (`mfr_path = "/a/b"`).
* ''A parent directory without one'': `409`, the body's `missing_parents` listing them root first (`["/docs", "/docs/notes"]`), and nothing written. With `"parents": true` they are created too.
* `400`: a path that does not exist, lies outside the repository's root, or is the root itself ([[Path representation]]); a `uuid` or an `mfr_path` in the same request (the path gives both).

!! Why the parents are asked for

Every recorded path hangs in the `mfr_path` forest, so a metarecord three levels down
needs the two directories above it. Creating them silently made a single request
write several metarecords nobody named — and, when they carried the target's
`mf_watch = false`, cut a whole subtree out of a watched area. They are now created
only on request, and as scaffolding: their stat fields and nothing else, so they
inherit `mf_watch` from above like any directory reconcile would have recorded.
